This project has two codelines, both TypeScript + vitest (ESM, `"type": "module"`):

- **mocka** at `/home/bradleyjerome/projects/mock3/mock-a` — source in `src/fares.ts`, tests in `test/fares.test.ts`. Public functions: `fareFor(rider: Rider): number` and `totalFor(riders: Rider[]): number`. The `Rider` type is `{ age: number; hasPass: boolean }`. Constants `BASE_FARE_CENTS` (350) and `CONCESSION_FARE_CENTS` (175) are exported. The doc comment states "Riders aged 65 AND OVER pay the concession fare," but the implementation uses `rider.age > 65` (strict greater-than), so the exact age-65 boundary is a known vulnerability surface. The child boundary uses `rider.age < 13` (strict less-than), so age 13 falls through to the base fare. Existing tests cover ages 8, 30, and 70 but NOT age 65 exactly, age 13 exactly, negative ages, or non-integer ages. When proposing fuzz cases for `fareFor`, pay special attention to the boundary at age 65 and age 13, negative ages, and non-integer ages.

- **mockb** at `/home/bradleyjerome/projects/mock3/mock-b` — source in `src/schedule.ts`, tests in `test/schedule.test.ts`. Public functions: `formatStops(stops: Stop[]): string` and `totalMinutes(stops: Stop[]): number`. The `Stop` type is `{ name: string; minutes: number }`. `formatStops` loops `for (let i = 0; i < stops.length - 1; i++)`, which drops the final stop — a known off-by-one vulnerability surface. Existing tests check that the output contains `'->'` and the first stop name but do NOT assert the presence of the last stop. When proposing fuzz cases for `formatStops`, pay special attention to single-element arrays, empty arrays, and whether every input stop appears in the output. For `totalMinutes`, note that it reads `stops[stops.length - 1].minutes`, which is consistent with the full stop list but inconsistent with what `formatStops` renders.

Tests in both codelines import from `../src/<module>` and use `describe`/`it`/`expect` from `vitest`. Both repos run tests with `vitest run` and type-check with `tsc --noEmit`.

